I can't speak to your time issues, but if you want to learn:

Hit Edit
Make some changes
Add a summary
Hit Show Preview
Check if it's good
Hit Save Page

A lot of the special markup is already contained in that page, so it
should be clear.  Wikia has a pretty decent sidebar with information
on the markup as well ("Editing tips" on the left).

(The documentation, particularly on Wikia, is an unfortunate
combination of difficult to find and somewhat obtuse, and it doesn't
help that the "Editing help" link at the bottom of the edit page leads
to nothing useful.  It would be great if someone took the time to
improve that.  I don't expect to be so inclined any time soon, so if
somebody is, they shouldn't wait on me.)

> Too, I'm guessing someone has a sense of ownership
> about that page, so I prefer nudging to bulldozing
> _in this case_.

Glancing at the edit history, I'm doubtful of that. And if someone
*does* have a sense of ownership of the page, they'll notice your edit
and do one of the following:

* observe that it's a good contribution and leave it
* observe that it's a good idea but could be rephrased to fit the rest
  of the article better and make a change to that end
* decide it's a bad edit and revert it

In the last two months, I've only seen reverts for two reasons: the
editor believes the information in the change being reverted to be
counter-factual, or the change being reverted had busted markup which
messed up the display of the page.

Wiki culture is entirely about the idea that if you see room for
improvement, you should go ahead and make the change.


